# 74. Artemis Operational Autonomy Classes

Operational Autonomy Classes classify **runtime Artemis actions**.

They are separate from the engineering Risk Tiers in Section 5.

An engineering work package may be Tier 3 while implementing a runtime action
that will later be classified as a low-autonomy operation, and vice versa.

## Class O0 — Observe

Artemis may:

- Read approved non-secret state
- Inspect health
- Research
- Compare options
- Produce recommendations
- Build Action Intents

No mutation is permitted.

## Class O1 — Autonomous Reversible Local Change

Artemis may execute automatically when all policy conditions pass.

Typical characteristics:

- Low blast radius
- Fully reversible
- No financial side effect
- No credential change
- No security-boundary change
- No external publication
- No irreversible deletion
- Deterministic validation exists

Possible examples after explicit registration:

- Add/remove a symbol from one analytical Agent within an approved universe
- Change an Agent timeframe within an approved set
- Change a non-security analytical preference
- Disable one unhealthy optional data source when a healthy fallback exists

## Class O2 — Bounded Autonomous Operational Change

Artemis may execute automatically only after the domain has been specifically
promoted for bounded autonomy and all limits pass.

Typical characteristics:

- Reversible or compensatable
- Moderate operational impact
- Bounded external calls
- Known owner and rollback
- Canary or validation path available
- No secret exposure
- No privilege escalation

Possible examples after verified maturity:

- Onboard an approved public read-only data source through the canonical source
  onboarding path
- Adjust bounded polling or routing configuration
- Move one approved analytical workload to a healthy provider
- Disable a degraded integration without deleting credentials

## Class O3 — Human-Approved Privileged Change

Artemis may research, plan, validate and prepare the action, but exact human
approval is required before execution.

Typical examples:

- Add a paid external provider
- Create or rotate provider credentials
- Change a setting with cross-system impact
- Modify important scheduler behavior
- Change a production integration with material cost or reliability impact
- Permanently delete operational data or a configured integration

## Class O4 — Critical / Strong Approval Change

Artemis may prepare the operation but must not autonomously approve it.

The policy may require step-up authentication, two-person approval, owner
approval or another independent control.

Examples include:

- Financial or account-changing operations governed by Sections 20 and 53
- Live-execution activation
- High-impact permission changes
- High-blast-radius destructive actions
- Security-control reconfiguration
- Critical production deployment actions where separately authorized

## Class O5 — Artemis-Prohibited Governance Action

These actions are outside autonomous Artemis authority even if technically
reachable.

Artemis may identify a need and prepare a proposal, but it may not grant itself
permission or execute the governance change autonomously.

Section 82 defines the canonical prohibited self-governance boundary.

## 74.1 Default-Deny Classification

An unclassified write action defaults to approval-required or prohibited
according to policy.

It must never default to autonomous execution.

## 74.2 Promotion and Demotion

An action class may be promoted to greater autonomy only through an approved
policy change supported by evidence.

Autonomy may be demoted immediately when:

- Failure rate increases
- Rollback fails
- Data quality degrades
- Provider behavior changes
- Security evidence becomes uncertain
- Unexpected blast radius is observed
- Audit lineage becomes incomplete

